use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dragging import Dragging
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence

export ui-component FloatingPanel:
    description:
        A floating panel is a small window inside the app that the user can
        move around and resize, such as a colour palette or a properties panel
        in a design tool. It floats above the page, which stays fully usable
        underneath.

    behaviors:
        - @{Dragging}
        - @{Presence}

    opening:
        The component decides what opens it, usually a button. It opens where
        the component says, such as next to the button, or in the middle of
        the screen. Focus moves into it, and goes back to the button when it
        closes. It has a title, which is its name for assistive tools.

    moving:
        The user drags it by its title bar. When the panel itself has focus,
        the arrow keys move it too, with a bigger step when holding Shift. It
        stays within the screen, or an area the component gives it, unless the
        component lets it go past the edges.

    resizing:
        The user drags its edges and corners to resize it. The component can
        set a smallest and largest size, keep its shape the same while it's
        resized, or stop it being resized at all.

    grid:
        The component can make moving and resizing snap to a grid.

    stages:
        Buttons in the title bar can shrink it down to just its title bar,
        grow it to fill the screen or its area, and put it back to the size it
        was. Double-clicking the title bar also fills the screen, or puts it
        back.

    reporting:
        The component is told as the panel moves and resizes, and once when the
        user lets go. It can also set the position and size itself.

    closing:
        A close button closes it. The component can let Escape close it too,
        while focus is inside. Clicking outside doesn't close it, because the
        page is meant to be used alongside it. The component decides whether
        it reopens where it was or where it first appeared.

    stacking:
        Several panels can be open at once. The one the user last used comes
        to the front, and Escape only closes that one.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't block the page. A window that does is a Dialog
        - Not attached to anything. A panel that follows something on the page
            is a Popover
        - It doesn't dock into the side of the layout
